## Agreement and corrections to the primary analysis

I agree with the fingerprint diagnosis and the recommendation to resume only
after configuration is settled. I do not adopt its categorical attribution to
an operator: the diff, timestamp and worker account establish an external edit
relative to the worker's reported writes, but do not identify the editor.

An isolated `.specrc` commit is not technically required. The operator may
commit all current reports/state alongside the intended configuration, as is
standard here. Such a commit does not fix or invalidate product verification;
the next successful boot recompiles and republishes the receipt. No manual
receipt edit or rollback of the worker's handoff is warranted.

The previous 0069 receipt incident was caused by tooling-owned registry
initialization and was repaired. This incident concerns a different input and
does not establish that repair regressed. The 0160-01 schema repair and
0160-02 origin/scope repair also remain intact. Earlier local validation of all
15 gates, 12 analyzed projects and 1,136 constructor cases remains historical
evidence; this interrupted run supplied no new gate verdict.

## Recommended course of action — awaiting agreement

1. Settle the backend choice while the arc is stopped. Preserve the current
   `claude-code` selection if that edit was intended; otherwise restore the
   intended selection explicitly. Normalize `.specrc` to LF as required by the
   repository. Do not infer permission to reverse the concurrent edit.
2. Let the operator commit the configuration and any desired escalation
   artifacts. No ASG/STL source repair, product edit, ledger amendment or gate
   change is necessary to unblock this incident. Preserve the origin record,
   counters, inherited floor and missing checkpoint for the supervisor to own.
3. On the operator's relaunch request, resume normally with the existing
   `.artifacts/0160-02-operator-repair/operator-reply.md` and
   `--executability-profile 1`, following manuals 012 and 013. Do not use
   `--accept-current-slice`: measured checkpoint 159 and case evidence are still
   required. Boot must successfully compile and publish a receipt for the settled
   configuration. Leave receipt-bound configuration/prompts unchanged during
   that run; make further backend switches between stopped runs under the
   present implementation.
4. Verify normal review and all 15 gates, including actual elevation coverage
   from `f395597c071910aaa215518626563f6c784bfb62`, measured 1,136 constructor
   cases and the exact census. Require checkpoint 159 before advancing to 0161.
   Reattach the terminal-event monitor. No separate repetition of the prior
   product tests is needed before that normal supervisor verification.

If live backend switching is a required workflow, address it explicitly in ASG:
identify changed receipt components in the error, distinguish dispatch settings
from contract-affecting `.specrc` settings, and define when a backend change
takes effect. Do not remove `.specrc` wholesale from validation. Such a change
needs tests proving backend-only switches preserve execution proofs while gate,
scope and other semantic changes still invalidate them. This is an alternative
tooling enhancement requiring agreement, not a prerequisite for the recovery
above and not work performed by this review. STL remains untouched.
